WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.290
Film Courage: Shane in your new book What
You Don’t Learn In Film School was there

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:06.390
a chapter or section that sparked controversy?

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.730
Shane Stanley, Filmmaker/Author/Instructor:
Oh absolutely.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:15.510
I think the most controversial chapter in
the book is the one that is the most important

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:20.269
is to get out, dig ditches, get your hands
dirty and make yourself available and whatever

00:00:20.269 --> 00:00:26.789
it takes to get on a set within your morals,
values, and ethics parameter to go do it.

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:33.470
And when I came up as we talked about how
I made phone calls and hustled to get work

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.740
and get on sets, I worked for free, I brought
my own lunch.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.730
I showed up first and left last.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.670
I was sweeping floors when it wasn’t in
my job description and I have close friends

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:49.190
that our generation grew up…you know I have
a friend whose father one two oscars and his

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:52.950
mother has nine Emmys…you want to talk about
nepotism?

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:56.580
And when he got his act together like I did
he said Hey you know you want to work in this

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:01.620
business I want to be an editor, mom picked
up the phone, made a phone call “Okay, he’ll

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:02.620
be there tomorrow.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.219
Two o’clock, perfect, thank you.”

00:01:05.219 --> 00:01:06.219
Click.

00:01:06.219 --> 00:01:07.219
“You’ve got a job.

00:01:07.219 --> 00:01:09.079
You’re sweeping floors at color.

00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:11.979
You’re getting paid nothing, pack a lunch,
dear.”

00:01:11.979 --> 00:01:18.299
And he swept floors for two years and he didn’t
have a problem with that.

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:20.729
He thought that’s normal.

00:01:20.729 --> 00:01:24.341
I had won Emmys at a young age and started
over and we talked about not being able to

00:01:24.341 --> 00:01:25.341
make rent?

00:01:25.341 --> 00:01:27.630
Where there was something called Crew Call
back then when I was coming up and you would

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.810
literally subscribe for like $50 a month,
you would call an 800 number, and you would

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:38.719
say press one for camera, two for sound, three
for electric and you would press these things

00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:42.560
that you could do and then you would hear
about jobs and you would write it down and

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.060
it would be a fax number and you would fax
your resume.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:50.679
You would spend a day a week doing that and
pray your phone rang and sometimes it wouldn’t.

00:01:50.679 --> 00:01:55.740
So a friend would call and say Hey I need
a swing in the camera department tomorrow.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:56.740
I pay lunch.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:57.740
You in it or out?

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.340
And I was like “Yeah, I’m there.”

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.109
The biggest problem I have today is when I
talk to film schools or when people read that

00:02:03.109 --> 00:02:06.610
chapter about having to invest in yourself
and build your resume.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:13.500
You know so many people in my opinion in film
school are sold on hopes and dreams and being

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:19.040
the next Tarantino or Damien Chazelle and
my advice is well if that doesn’t happen,

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:20.420
what’s the game plan?

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:25.690
So get out, learn camera, learn sound, learn
editing, learn grip, learn gaff, learn AC,

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:30.860
whatever you can and if somebody can’t pay
you to do something and you have an opportunity

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:36.989
to make new contacts, learn and be on a set,
something good will come from that.

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:41.849
Would you rather sit home and play on your
phone, play Xbox or watch repeated streaming

00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:43.220
shows that you’ve seen six times?

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:48.739
You want to get out and make new contacts
and I find a lot of people think that’s

00:02:48.739 --> 00:02:50.170
not the way to do it.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:55.840
And I also think well okay over 85% of people
who spend money and graduate from film schools

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.750
never earn a dime in our industry and there’s
a reason for it and that’s because they’re

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:05.270
uneducated on how to make their lives work
and how to build a career base and contacts

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:08.560
and become steady earners in our business
and the way did it and the way I know a lot

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.860
of people who did it was they got out there
and they proved their worth by hustling.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.760
And some of the best workers like we just
finished a film called BREAK EVEN that comes

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.400
out, some of the best workers on that film
were the interns from [Los Angeles] Pierce

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.400
College.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.510
There’s a kid that stands out.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:32.400
He worked so hard every day, never said a
word, head down, he anticipated everybody’s

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.599
needs, he was the guy that was cleaning up
everybody’s trash at the end of the night,

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:40.329
showed up on days he wasn’t supposed to
be there just because he wanted to check in

00:03:40.329 --> 00:03:43.340
saying I’m coming in tomorrow, does anybody
need anything.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:47.299
What I always tell people is when you make
a movie and you’re trying to carve your

00:03:47.299 --> 00:03:52.870
way, make it so the decision makers don’t
want to make a movie without you and every

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:55.890
time you do a film you sure meet a lot of
people you never want to make another movie

00:03:55.890 --> 00:04:01.459
with but it’s nice when you see those one
or two diamond in the rough that you say “I

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:03.180
want to bring them back.”

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:08.249
And they were somebody who was a PA, they
were an assistant, they were a runner.

00:04:08.249 --> 00:04:14.439
I think if people take that approach as I
say in the book whenever you offer somebody

00:04:14.439 --> 00:04:15.989
a job it’s “What’s my credit?

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:16.989
What’s my pay?”

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:19.920
It’s always the first question “What’s
my credit?

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.639
What’s my pay?”

00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:28.220
And I think it needs to be okay…take a breath
and think about what the experience will do

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:29.220
for you.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:33.120
In the same chapter I talk about the best
thing I ever did for my career was say yes

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.840
to something I knew nothing about, I did it
for free and it took up a month an a half

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.070
of my life and that was going to Bally, Indonesia
with Zalman King when he directed IN GOD’S

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:43.070
HANDS.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:46.940
Zalman had seen my work ethic around a sounds
stage he owned in Canoga Park [Los Angeles,

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:47.940
California].

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.960
I was sleeping floors and he walked in and
he goes I thought you were producing or doing

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.060
something more important.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:53.710
I said “Yeah, everybody left.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:55.680
I wanted to leave your studio the way we found
it.”

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:56.680
And he says “What’s your name?”

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:58.720
And he came over and talked to me.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.160
We befriended each other and visited on set
and the next thing you know he called me up

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:05.080
and said Hey, what are you doing in November
or December?

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.000
I said “I don’t know, what do you got?”

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:11.240
And he said Well I’m doing a film in Bally,
I think you should come.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:12.240
I said Do What?

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.860
And he said Shadow me, learn.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:20.150
He says I’ll offer you a free education
working with me and we’ll feed you and travel

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:21.259
you, you figure out the rest.

00:05:21.259 --> 00:05:24.620
He didn’t pay me a dime and it was like
talk about film school.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:29.330
You’re sitting next to Zalman friggin’
King as he is directing a movie for a major

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:33.009
studio and I was on his hip for a month and
a half doing that.

00:05:33.009 --> 00:05:35.880
I learned more by just saying yes.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.380
I didn’t ask would I get a credit?

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:41.380
Would I get paid?

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:44.990
They limo-ed me to the airport and first class
me round trip and gave me my own bungalow

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:46.199
in a five-story resort.

00:05:46.199 --> 00:05:48.300
I didn’t ask for any of that.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.631
For all I knew I’d be sleeping in a tube
tent on the side of the mountain, but they

00:05:51.631 --> 00:05:52.650
took care of me.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.129
And I think that’s the attitude.

00:05:55.129 --> 00:05:56.889
Obviously you don’t want to be taken advantage
of.

00:05:56.889 --> 00:06:03.479
You don’t want to get into a situation that’s
not healthy but there seems to be such resistance

00:06:03.479 --> 00:06:08.520
in getting out and experiencing work without
worrying about pay.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.150
There’s too many people that will do it.

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:15.229
Film Courage: Do you think that’s because
for our generation (Gen X) that there wasn’t

00:06:15.229 --> 00:06:22.080
the term “intern” and let’s play the
devil’s advocate…yes interns can be taken

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:26.861
advantage of in terms of people using free
labor and stringing them along and not [eventually]

00:06:26.861 --> 00:06:27.940
giving them a job.

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:29.560
Is that what they’re afraid of?

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:30.560
Shane: I don’t know.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:36.790
I think honestly as a society we have become
pretty tender, I think we’ve become very

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:37.790
sensitive.

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:41.490
I think people are afraid of exploitation
which I think is something we all need to

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:48.419
consider but I know I’ve got a nephew who
worked is you-know-what off working for a

00:06:48.419 --> 00:06:54.270
financial institution in Arizona (Scottsdale),
working 18 hours a day, getting their lunch,

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:55.879
cleaning up their mess, getting them coffee.

00:06:55.879 --> 00:07:01.490
He didn’t get paid much at all but now he’s
an actual worker in that industry and he used

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:04.210
that after college, it wasn’t for college
credit.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:08.460
He graduated college, his dad made a few phone
calls and said you’re going to go sweat,

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.550
you’re going to wear a tie and you’re
going to go ride their wave, you’re not

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:13.600
going to get paid, you’re going to learn.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:19.080
It happens in other industries but it just
seem in ours I think it’s because the hours

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.729
are abused.

00:07:20.729 --> 00:07:24.620
In a normal workplace it’s an 8-hours workday
with an hour break.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:28.620
In film it’s 10 to 12 if you’re lucky
and I think people are afraid they’re going

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.800
to be working 16-18 hours a day as free labor.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.919
So it’s hard to say why but I never questioned
that.

00:07:35.919 --> 00:07:40.360
Maybe it was just the fact that I could be
on a set and make connections and learn and

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.180
I always figured well I could sit at home
and wait for the phone to ring or I could

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:51.440
be on a set and meet a director or a producer
who may find my job skills necessary to bring

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.370
me to the next journey and then make a great
connection and say “By the way I have a

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:56.370
script.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:08:00.380
By the way I also do camera, can I get an
opportunity.”

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:01.840
And it doesn’t happen by sitting at home,
it just doesn’t.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:02.840
Film Courage: Great…excellent.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:05.750
Shane: At least I don’t think it does.

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:13.159
I mean you can upload to Youtube and Vimeo
until you’re blue in the face.

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:16.580
Film Courage: Yes but working around people
is a different experience absolutely.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:17.789
There is good and bad with that.

00:08:17.789 --> 00:08:22.550
Shane: The best gigs and best opportunities
I ever had in my life were showing up and

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:28.379
working hard and proving myself as a worker
who was competent and the people in the decision-making

00:08:28.379 --> 00:08:32.050
capacities going out of their way to say Hey…you
know…I appreciate your work today.

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:33.050
Thank you.

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:37.729
We’ve got something coming up next week,
I want to get you on that and then they start

00:08:37.729 --> 00:08:42.680
to appreciate you and you work these gigs
and the next thing you know you have an audience,

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:47.560
you have an ear with the decision makers and
when you want to move up in the world or they

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.710
know people who will benefit by knowing you.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:50.710
That’s all it is in this business.

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:51.710
Question for the Viewers: When starting out
what matters more ‘credit and pay’ or

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:51.711
‘experience?’

